    "result_text": "All 3 tests pass with a clean build. Let me summarize the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nTelephone numbers are now stored and returned in **E.164 form**, replacing the old \"exactly 10 digits\" behavior.\n\n**Core logic \u2014 `TelephoneNormalizer.java`**\n- `canonicalize(String)` (never rejects, used for comparison and stored form): strips all non-digit characters (spaces, dashes, brackets, etc.). If the input starts with `+`, the country code is kept as-is; otherwise `+61` is assumed and a single leading `0` is dropped from the national digits. Returns `\"+\" + digits`.\n- `normalize(String)` (used on create): calls `canonicalize`, then requires **8\u201315 digits after the `+`**, throwing `InvalidOwnerFieldsException` (\u2192 400) otherwise.\n- Duplicate detection in `OwnerRestControllerV1` already compared `normalize(new)` against `canonicalize(existing)` \u2014 since both now yield the same E.164 string, duplicates are detected on E.164 values with no controller change needed.\n\n**Supporting changes**\n- `Owner.java`: replaced the `@Digits(integer=10)` / `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")` entity constraints with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` so the persisted E.164 value validates (dropped the now-unused `@Digits` import). This was essential \u2014 the old pattern would have rejected `+61412345678` on flush.\n- `openapi.yml`: updated the `telephone` field description and example to document the E.164 behavior.\n\n**Verification against the tests**\n- `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678` \u2713\n- `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456` (explicit country code kept) \u2713\n- `12` \u2192 `+6112` (4 digits < 8) \u2192 400 \u2713\n- `uniqueTelephone()` (`04XXXXXXXX`) \u2192 `+614XXXXXXXX` (11 digits), valid across the rule chain \u2713\n\nBuild is green: `Tests run: 3, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`.",
